Under the Public Health Service Act, 2075, the provisions regarding informed consent are set out as follows:

* **Requirement for Consent:** Every health institution must obtain the informed consent of the service recipient while providing him or her with health service [E2]. Providing treatment service without obtaining informed consent pursuant to Section 11 is prohibited [E3].
* **Definition:** "Informed consent" means written or oral consent given by a person who is legally competent to obtain health service [E1], [E2].
* **Prescribed Treatment:** Written consent must be obtained to provide the prescribed treatment [E1], [E2].
* **Exceptions (Treatment Without Informed Consent):** Health service may be provided without the informed consent of the service recipient in any of the following circumstances [E1]:
  * If the service recipient is not in a condition to give consent or has not authorized anyone else to give consent, and consent is instead given by his or her wife or husband, father or mother, grandfather or grandmother, adult son or daughter, brother or sister (as available respectively), or the available closest person of the service recipient [E1];
  * If the health service is provided without consent under prevailing law or by an order of the court [E1];
  * If there is a serious threat likely to occur upon public health if the person is not treated [E1];
  * If there is a possibility of death of the patient or irreparable damage to his or her health is likely to occur if the provision of health service is delayed [E1];
  * If the concerned person has not refused to obtain health service in an expressed or unexpressed manner or by conduct [E1];
  * In other cases as prescribed [E1].

